"""
FishScan7 disease information.

This module uses the exact data schema expected by ui.py:
display_name, what_is_it, symptoms, treatment, and reference.

The model predicts a dataset class from an image. It does not establish a
clinical diagnosis, identify a pathogen, or determine the cause of a lesion.
Treatment information is therefore framed as potential management or
treatment considerations rather than a prescription.
"""

DISEASE_INFORMATION = {'Bacterial Red disease': {'display_name': 'Bacterial Red Disease', 'what_is_it': 'A bacterial disease category characterized by reddish or hemorrhagic changes of the skin and other external tissues. The appearance can overlap with several bacterial disease conditions, so the image pattern alone does not establish a specific bacterial species or cause.', 'symptoms': ['Reddish or hemorrhagic areas of the skin', 'Skin erosions or ulcers', 'Localized tissue damage', 'Fin damage or necrotic areas', 'Lethargy or other signs of systemic disease'], 'treatment': 'Management depends on the underlying cause and severity. Common priorities include maintaining suitable water quality, reducing environmental stress, isolating affected fish when appropriate, and investigating the bacterial cause. Antibacterial treatment should be selected only after appropriate clinical assessment and, where feasible, identification of the causative organism and antimicrobial susceptibility.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Bacterial diseases - Aeromoniasis': {'display_name': 'Aeromoniasis', 'what_is_it': 'A bacterial disease associated with Aeromonas species and commonly involving the skin, underlying tissues, and sometimes internal organs. Clinical presentation varies with the bacterial strain, fish species, environmental conditions, and severity of infection. Visual findings alone cannot confirm Aeromonas infection.', 'symptoms': ['Skin reddening or hemorrhagic lesions', 'Skin erosions or ulcers', 'Fin damage', 'Lethargy or reduced activity', 'Abdominal swelling or other systemic signs'], 'treatment': 'Management should begin with correction of environmental conditions and reduction of stress, together with isolation or other biosecurity measures when indicated. Antibacterial therapy may be considered after the bacterial cause has been evaluated. Selection of an antimicrobial should take account of the confirmed or suspected organism and available susceptibility information.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Bacterial gill disease': {'display_name': 'Bacterial Gill Disease', 'what_is_it': 'A bacterial disease condition primarily affecting the gills and therefore interfering with normal respiratory function. Bacterial gill disease has been associated with organisms such as Flavobacterium branchiophilum, but the model prediction alone does not confirm a particular bacterial agent.', 'symptoms': ['Rapid or difficult breathing', 'Increased respiratory effort', 'Excess mucus on the gills', 'Abnormal gill appearance', 'Lethargy or reduced activity'], 'treatment': 'Management focuses on improving water quality, maintaining adequate dissolved oxygen, reducing crowding and other stressors, and addressing sources of gill damage. Additional treatment depends on the confirmed cause, fish species, production system, and local veterinary or aquatic health guidance.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Fungal diseases Saprolegniasis': {'display_name': 'Saprolegniasis', 'what_is_it': 'An aquatic oomycete disease commonly associated with Saprolegnia species. It often develops on damaged, stressed, or otherwise compromised tissues and may produce characteristic cottony growths. Although it is commonly described as a fungal disease, Saprolegnia is an oomycete rather than a true fungus.', 'symptoms': ['White, grey, or cottony growth on affected tissue', 'Cottony masses on the skin or other external surfaces', 'Expanding superficial skin lesions', 'Tissue damage associated with previous injury or stress', 'Reduced activity in more severely affected fish'], 'treatment': 'Management should address underlying stress, injury, poor water quality, or other predisposing conditions. Isolation and careful husbandry may help limit progression. Any chemical or bath treatment should be selected according to fish species, life stage, water system, local regulations, and an appropriate aquatic health assessment.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Healthy Fish': {'display_name': 'Healthy Fish', 'what_is_it': 'A reference category representing fish images without obvious external disease signs represented by the dataset. A model prediction of this class does not establish that a fish is clinically healthy, because internal disease, early disease, or subtle abnormalities may not be visible in an image.', 'symptoms': ['No obvious external lesions represented by the dataset', 'No obvious ulceration or severe skin damage', 'No obvious abnormal growth on the external surface', 'No obvious severe gill abnormality in the available image', 'No obvious external signs assigned to another disease class'], 'treatment': 'No disease-specific treatment is indicated from this image category alone. Preventive fish health management includes appropriate water quality, nutrition, stocking conditions, biosecurity, and routine observation. Further examination is appropriate when abnormal behaviour or other concerns are present.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Parasitic diseases': {'display_name': 'Parasitic Diseases', 'what_is_it': 'A broad category covering diseases caused by parasites that may affect the skin, gills, or internal organs. Different parasites produce different clinical signs and require different diagnostic and treatment approaches, so this category should not be interpreted as identification of a particular parasite.', 'symptoms': ['Excessive mucus production', 'Skin irritation or abnormal coloration', 'Flashing, rubbing, or other irritation-related behaviour', 'Rapid or difficult breathing when gills are affected', 'Skin or gill lesions'], 'treatment': 'Treatment depends on the parasite involved and should follow appropriate identification or clinical assessment. Management may include improving water quality, reducing stress, isolating affected fish, and using a parasite-specific treatment when indicated. Different parasite groups require different therapies, so a generic treatment should not be inferred from an image.', 'reference': 'Noga (2010); Biswas et al. (2024).'}, 'Viral diseases White tail disease': {'display_name': 'White Tail Disease', 'what_is_it': 'A dataset category associated with White Tail Disease and characterized in reported cases by abnormal whitening and degeneration of muscle tissue, particularly in the tail and abdominal regions. In this application, the label is used for image classification and should not be interpreted as confirmation of a viral infection or identification of a specific causative agent.', 'symptoms': ['Whitish or opaque muscle in the tail region', 'Whitish appearance of abdominal muscle', 'Muscle degeneration or necrosis', 'Lethargy or reduced activity', 'Increased mortality in affected populations'], 'treatment': 'No established antiviral treatment is provided by this image classification result. Management of confirmed outbreaks primarily emphasizes biosecurity, isolation or removal of affected stocks when appropriate, sanitation and disinfection, and prevention of pathogen introduction or spread. Laboratory testing is required when confirmation of the underlying viral infection is necessary.', 'reference': 'Owens et al. (2009); Sahul Hameed et al. (2013); WOAH (2024).'}}

